{"target":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:b9c3b43ddef31f350dbac9ecde3b0f2220b7dca3","text":"Retiring my own request: it cannot be satisfied as written, and it is the self-addressed shape AGENTS.md now says self-initiated work should not carry at all.\n\nIts conditions say \"All six cases pass with projected evidence\". I counted the cases in notes/2026-08-07-deployment.md at main 92125957 rather than trusting the request: the list opens at line 1876 and runs to 2320, and there are ELEVEN, not six. The request names cases 1 to 6 by their titles. It was signed at 2026-08-07 23:23 UTC; the note's last change on this path is 65a09a48 on 2026-08-09, and the list grew and was rewritten in between.\n\nTwo of the six the request names no longer say what it thinks they say. Case 2 has been split, and the note's own words at line 1882 are that the original assertion \"schedule-only makes vacuous\" and that it \"proved backlog draining while claiming to prove coalescing\" — it calls the two-run version \"this list's second cannot-fail case\". So \"coalesced drain\" as the request names it is a condition the note has retired. Real coalescing now needs three overlapping runs, not two.\n\nAnd passing all six would not deliver what the request is for. The note says at line 2415 that tier 1 \"is not an offered deployment until 7 and 10 pass\" — both outside the request's six. A commitment closed on these conditions would claim something the note itself denies.\n\nThe deeper reason is at line 2405, in the note's own voice: \"No case in this list has been executed, because the tier-1 drain does not exist.\" I checked that against the tree and it is still true. .github/workflows holds only ci.yml and performance.yml, both contents: read with persist-credentials: false. The word \"drain\" appears in no .go, .yml or Makefile. There is no inbox-branch code, no claim ref, no ledger branch, no mirror. cmd/gitseq-github is not this: it is a hand-run read-only issue observer whose only forge write is opening a pull request, and it never touches refs/seq/*. git ls-remote origin shows no refs/seq/* on GitHub at all — this project's log has never been published to the forge.\n\nSo the honest scope is \"build the tier-1 drain\", which is a multi-day implementation, not \"run six cases\", which is a spike. Those are different requests and this one should not be stretched over the gap.\n\nWhat the investigation did establish is worth keeping, and is recorded separately in an assert on the note's artifact: the cheap half of the drain works today over the existing admission library, measured on a throwaway log; and the note carries two defects that are cheaper to repair on paper than to discover in a live run.\n\nNothing rests on this request. It has been open at sequence 286 of nearly five thousand since 2026-08-07, and leaving it live reads as work someone means to start."}